  <h1>One radio, and a second shared by everyone in range.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    One radio and one antenna. Every device in range divides each second the same way.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h3 id="second">Where the second goes.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    The device has one radio and one antenna, so it divides each second into
    time slots, each on one frequency. GPS marks the start of each second to
    the microsecond. Every skyBlip Go, every skyPost and every
    FLARM-generation device in range divides the same second the same way.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="modules">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Time since the start of the second</th><th>Tuned to</th><th>What is on the channel</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>0 to 200&nbsp;ms</th><td>868.400&nbsp;MHz</td><td>the end of the time slot that began at 800&nbsp;ms in the previous second. FLARM-generation traffic is still transmitting here, so the device keeps listening. Receive only</td></tr>
        <tr><th>205 to 395&nbsp;ms</th><td>869.525&nbsp;MHz</td><td>the skyPost uplink from the ground station: the ADS-B traffic it received and the weather data it holds, at 200&nbsp;kbit/s. Receive only, always</td></tr>
        <tr><th>400 to 800&nbsp;ms</th><td>868.200&nbsp;MHz</td><td>direct traffic on the lower channel, ADS-L and FLARM-generation together</td></tr>
        <tr><th>800 to 1200&nbsp;ms</th><td>868.400&nbsp;MHz</td><td>the same on the upper channel, continuing 200&nbsp;ms into the next second</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="lede">
    Listening on both 868 channels matters. ADS-L alternates channels
    from one transmission to the next. A FLARM-generation device transmits
    on the lower channel in the first half of the second and the upper in the
    second half. A receiver that stayed on one channel would hear half of its
    neighbours and never know which half.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    On those two channels the device receives two systems in one time slot. ADS-L and
    the FLARM-generation format both precede their frames with a 32-bit sync word,
    a signal pattern. The two words share their first eighteen chips, or signal elements.
    The receiver detects this shared sequence, and the software identifies the system
    from the chips that follow. The few milliseconds either side of the 869.525&nbsp;MHz slot are
    the margin a band change needs, because the two bands are two modulations
    and not two frequencies: 100&nbsp;kbit/s Manchester FSK below,
    200&nbsp;kbit/s GMSK above.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    The transmit window is shorter. The device transmits on the 868 channels only,
    between 450 and 1000&nbsp;ms, on whichever of the two channels is assigned
    to that second. It always transmits ADS-L: the FLARM-generation format
    is received, never transmitted. The ground station transmits on
    869.525&nbsp;MHz. This device only listens there and never
    transmits on that frequency. Its transmission time within the slot is calculated from
    the device address and the current second, then recalculated every second. This
    keeps two aircraft whose transmissions overlapped once from overlapping again.
  </p>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>GPS aligns four time slots a second to the microsecond</li>
    <li>The device uses 868.200 and 868.400&nbsp;MHz for 400&nbsp;ms each</li>
    <li>It listens for the ground station on 869.525&nbsp;MHz for 190&nbsp;ms</li>
    <li>It decodes two systems within one synchronisation window</li>
    <li>It transmits for about 5&nbsp;ms, at a time no received signal can change</li>
    <li>It may transmit for 1% of the hour and uses half of that</li>
  </ul>
</section>

